1. First, ensure you have a User model in your Laravel application. If not, create one using the `make:model` Artisan command:

```
php artisan make:model User
```

2. Next, define the function in your controller. For example, if you have a `UserController`, you can create a `show` method:

```php
namespace App\Http\Controllers;

use App\User;
use Illuminate\Http\Request;
use Illuminate\Routing\Controller;

class UserController extends Controller
{
    public function show($id)
    {
        // Retrieve the user from the database
        $user = User::find($id);

        // Check if the user was found
        if (!$user) {
            return response()->json(['error' => 'User not found'], 404);
        }

        // Return the user as a JSON response
        return response()->json($user);
    }
}
```

3. Register the route for the function in your `routes/web.php` file:

```php
Route::get('/user/{id}', 'UserController@show');
```

Now, when you access the `/user/{id}` endpoint, Laravel will call the `show` function in the `UserController`, which retrieves the user with the provided ID from the database and returns the result as a JSON response. If the user is not found, it will return a 404 error with an error message.

